<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plautus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 254–184 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Titus Maccius Plautus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plautus, in full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a> Maccius Plautus, was a Roman comic playwright. His comedies, adapted from Greek New Comedy and set in Greek cities, are lively plays of scheming slaves, young lovers and misers, and twenty of them survive. They are the oldest complete works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> literature and influenced Shakespeare, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moliere">Molière</a> and later European comedy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plautus.jpg" alt="Plautus" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pierre François Barrois · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plautus_18th_century_engraving_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Contact with the Greek world transformed Roman culture: Livius Andronicus translated the Odyssey into Latin around 240 BCE, <b>Plautus</b> and Terence adapted Greek comedy, Ennius wrote the epic Annales, and in the late Republic Cicero, Lucretius, Catullus, Sallust and Caesar created a classical Latin prose and poetry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Livius Andronicus, a Greek from southern Italy, translated the Odyssey and staged the first Latin play adapted from a Greek original in 240 BCE; <b>Plautus</b> and Terence wrote comedies, and Ennius wrote an epic history of Rome in Greek metre.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman authors such as Sallust mention books in Punic, but none survive except in translation, as Mago’s treatise does, or in snippets, as in the plays of <b>Plautus</b>, whose Poenulus gives Punic words in Latin spelling, such as chyl, ‘all’.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
